question 1] Git tracks all the changes you make to your files over time.
1. Download Git from the official site: git-scm.com.
2. Open the installer file and click Next through all the default options. You do not need to change anything.
3. Once the installation is finished, open your terminal (PowerShell on Windows or Terminal on Mac) and type:bash
git --version

question 2] You need your terminal to look directly inside your project directory.
1. Open VS Code and open your web-development-practice folder.
2. Go to the top menu bar, click Terminal, and select New Terminal.
3. A terminal window will open at the bottom of VS Code, already inside your project folder path.

question 3] This command initializes a brand new, empty Git repository inside your project.
1. Type the following command in your terminal and press Enter:bash
git init

question 4] Git needs to know who is saving the work so your name is attached to your changes.
1. Set your name by running:bash
git config --global user.name "Your Name"

2. Set your email address by running:bash
git config --global user.email "your.email@example.com"

question 5] 1. In VS Code, open your index.html file (or create one if it is blank).
2. Add a basic title to it, like <h1>My Git Practice Page</h1>.
3. Check what Git sees by running:bash
git status
Use code with caution.
Your index.html file will be red, meaning it is untracked.
4. Stage the file to prepare it for saving:bash
git add index.html
Use code with caution.
5. Save the snapshot with a meaningful message:bash
git commit -m "Initial commit: Set up index.html structure"
Use code with caution.

